The server-side loot roll for one player looks like this.
```python
def roll_rarity():
    return choice(["common", "rare", "epic"], [60, 25, 8])

gold = 0
best = "common"
drop = "common"
big_drops = 0
for chest in range(3):
    drop = roll_rarity()
    if drop == "epic":
        big_drops += 1
        best = "epic"
        gold += 50
    elif drop == "rare":
        gold += 10
        if best != "epic":
            best = "rare"
    else:
        gold += randint(1, 2)
if big_drops == 0:
    if flip(0.4):
        big_drops += 1
        best = "epic"
```
Here `flip(p)` returns True with probability p; `randint(a, b)` draws an integer between a and b, inclusive, uniformly; `choice(xs, w)` returns xs[i] with probability w[i] / sum(w) (uniform when no weights are given); `observe(c)` is a hard constraint; a run that reaches it with c false is thrown away (rejection sampling). Each random call is independent of all others.
We execute the program one time. After the program, additional observe lines are appended one at a time; each one further conditions the same run. Answer about the final values of the variables.

